---
title: "Hire when the machine is already running"
description: "Headcount is not growth. It's a bet you place after the system works."
date: "2026-07-18"
tags:
  - hiring
  - scaling
published: true
layout: docs
---

I said this publicly: when Algility hits $1M ARR, I hire the most cracked engineers I can find.

People thought it was ego. It was a buying rule.

Most teams hire to feel progress. Empty calendar. Panic. Post a role. Now payroll is the product. Work gets slower. Quality dips. Six months later you "manage someone out" of a job that never should have existed.

That is not leadership. That is expensive avoidance.

My rule is ugly and simple.

1. Automate the repeat before you staff it.
2. Hire only when a human is the bottleneck, not your loneliness.
3. Fire yourself from work a system should own.
4. Kill offers, channels, and clients that only work if you babysit them.

Algility stays small on purpose until the machine is loud. Then headcount makes it sharper. Not noisier.

Hiring scales a machine. It does not invent one. If you hire first, you bought a payroll problem and called it a company.
